@page
@model Parley.Web.Pages.JoinModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = Model.Current is null ? "Join" : $"Joined as {Model.Current.Person}";
}

@* The one page where the full mark earns its room. It is the door a person comes in through,
   and every other page in the console is a working view that would be worse for a logo on it. *@
<div class="doorway">
    <img class="wordmark" src="/brand/wordmark.png" alt="" width="453" height="480" />
    <h1>@(Model.Current is null ? "Join this deployment" : $"You are here as {Model.Current.Person}")</h1>
</div>

<p class="lede">
    Type a name and you become an agent like any other: a registration in each project, a place
    in each roster, and an identity recorded against everything you say. SPEC.md §11.2 has always
    allowed a person to be a responder and §11.3 has always given them a name — what was missing
    was somewhere to hold it while you sit here, which is what a seat is.
</p>

@if (Model.Problem is { Length: > 0 } problem)
{
    <p class="unreachable" role="alert">@problem</p>
}

@if (Model.Outcomes.Count > 0)
{
    <h2>What happened</h2>

    <ul class="points">
        @foreach (var outcome in Model.Outcomes)
        {
            <li class="@(outcome.Seated ? "open" : "ghosted")">
                <span class="label">@outcome.Project</span>
                <span class="point-text">@outcome.Says</span>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}

@if (Model.Current is null)
{
    <h2>Pick a name</h2>

    <p>
        It becomes <code>&lt;name&gt;@@@Parley.Core.MachineName.Current</code> — the machine half
        is this console's and not yours to choose, because a seat is taken where you are sitting.
        Lower case, starting with a letter, letters and digits and hyphens, up to 32 characters.
        Your own first name is the usual answer.
    </p>

    <p>
        A name an agent already holds will be refused rather than taken over. That is the one
        thing joining can do that an operator never could: speaking as somebody puts words in a
        transcript under their name, and an operator instructing an agent has always been visibly
        the operator doing it.
    </p>

    <form method="post">
        <input type="hidden" name="Then" value="@Model.Then" />
        <label for="person">Name</label>
        <input id="person" name="Person" value="@Model.Person" autocomplete="given-name"
               spellcheck="false" autofocus />
        <button type="submit">Join</button>
    </form>

    @if (Model.Projects.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="unreachable">
            This machine has no projects, so there is nothing to join. Name one with
            <code>parley project add</code> first.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>
            Joining seats you in @(Model.Projects.Count == 1 ? "this project" : "all of these"),
            because a person is at a console rather than at a page:
        </p>

        <ul class="points">
            @foreach (var project in Model.Projects)
            {
                <li>
                    <span class="label">@project.Name.Value</span>
                    <span class="point-text">@project.Endpoint</span>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    }
}
else
{
    <h2>Where you are seated</h2>

    @if (Model.Held.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="unreachable">
            The console remembers your name and is holding no seat anywhere — every project's
            broker was unreachable, or released the seat while nobody was using the console.
            Joining again takes it back.
        </p>

        <form method="post">
            <input type="hidden" name="Person" value="@Model.Current.Person" />
            <button type="submit">Take my seat again</button>
        </form>
    }
    else
    {
        <ul class="points">
            @foreach (var held in Model.Held)
            {
                <li class="open">
                    <span class="label">@held.Project</span>
                    <span class="point-text">
                        @* No count here. It belongs on one page, asked of the broker once — a
                           second place showing it is a second thing that can be wrong, which is
                           exactly what went wrong when the frame kept its own. *@
                        @held.Name.Value, since @held.Since.ToLocalTime().ToString("HH:mm")
                    </span>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>

        <p>
            Your seat is an open connection, which is what SPEC.md §4.0 makes presence out of:
            there is no heartbeat and nothing to expire, so holding the connection is being here
            and dropping it is being gone. Leaving releases it in every project at once. So does
            @SeatIdle minutes with nobody using this console — a tab left open is not a person at
            a desk, and a roster that said otherwise would be lying to the agents reading it.
        </p>
    }
}

@functions {
    /// <summary>How long a seat survives an unused console, in whole minutes.</summary>
    private static int SeatIdle => (int)Parley.Client.Seating.SeatBook.IdleAfter.TotalMinutes;
}
